_Answers and work are shown in the red blocks._

== Butterbeer

Preferences for butter beer can be represented by the following demand curve:

#mitex(`
P_d = 100 - \frac{1}{2} Q_d
`)

The supply curve for butter beer can be represented by the following equation:

#mitex(`
P = 20 + \frac{1}{2} Q
`)

Prices are in galleons and quantity is in bottles.

== Q1 | {++Opening the Market++}

The Ministry of Magic hired an external committee of advisors #strike[to find means by which to raise government revenue without raising taxes]. One classically trained magical economist proposed opening the closed domestic butterbeer market to the less expensive global market (#mi(`P = 40`)) #strike[while imposing an import tariff of 2 galleons]. Ministry officials were pleased with the option but also raised concerns that domestic butterbeer sellers would be harmed by the lower price. {++Use a graph and algebra to analyze the impact.++}

a) Imports or exports? *imports*, exports

b) Quantity demanded: *120* Quantity supplied: *40* Imports: *80*

c) Consumer surplus before: *1600* after: *3600*

d) Producer surplus before: *1600* after: *400*

e) Change in total surplus: *+800*

f) Buyers: *gain*, lose Sellers: gain, *lose*

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      Closed market: 80 bottles at 60 galleons, CS = PS = 1600.

      At the world price of 40, below the domestic price, buyers buy abroad: the market *imports*.

      Quantity demanded 120, quantity supplied 40; imports 120 − 40 = *80*.

      CS = ½ · 120 · (100 − 40) = *3600* (+2000). PS = ½ · 40 · (40 − 20) = *400* (−1200).

      Total surplus rises by *800*, the triangle ½ · 80 · |60 − 40| between the curves and the world price. Buyers gain more than sellers lose.

      The officials were right: sellers lose 1200. But buyers gain 2000, so the market as a whole gains 800.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

== Q2 | An Alternative World

If instead of a price of 40 galleons in Question 1 the global price had been 80 what would have been the distributional impact of opening the domestic market for trade? {++Use a graph and algebra.++} #strike[No need to do math here. Simply use a graph to show the impact on the market.]

a) Imports or exports? imports, *exports*

b) Quantity demanded: *40* Quantity supplied: *120* Exports: *80*

c) Consumer surplus before: *1600* after: *400*

d) Producer surplus before: *1600* after: *3600*

e) Change in total surplus: *+800*

f) Buyers: gain, *lose* Sellers: *gain*, lose

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      Closed market: 80 bottles at 60 galleons, CS = PS = 1600.

      At the world price of 80, above the domestic price, sellers sell abroad: the market *exports*.

      Quantity demanded 40, quantity supplied 120; exports 120 − 40 = *80*.

      CS = ½ · 40 · (100 − 80) = *400* (−1200). PS = ½ · 120 · (80 − 20) = *3600* (+2000).

      Total surplus rises by *800*, the triangle ½ · 80 · |60 − 80| between the curves and the world price. Sellers gain more than buyers lose.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

== Q3 | {++A Changing World Price++}

{++Return to Q1. Suppose the global price rises from 40 to 50 galleons.++}

a) Quantity demanded: *100* Quantity supplied: *60* Imports: *40*

b) Consumer surplus: *2500* Producer surplus: *900*

c) Imports have: risen, stayed the same, *fallen*

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  At 50, buyers want 100 and domestic sellers supply 60: imports fall from 80 to *40*.

  CS = ½ · 100 · (100 − 50) = *2500*, down from 3600; PS = ½ · 60 · (50 − 20) = *900*, up from 400. The gain from trade shrinks from 800 to 200: the closer the world price is to the domestic price, the less there is to gain.
]

